"""P2: the budget floor reaches zero, and the parser is pinned into the cache key.

Every outbound single from the real Grindleford page is fifty pence, and the
budget was floored at one pound in five places -- two input `min` attributes,
the clamp in `setBudget`, the guard in `applyState`, and the chooser's own
`resolvedBudget`. The slider's lowest position therefore still showed all
thirteen of those rows: the empty state, and the recovery moves that are its
entire purpose, could not be reached on that page at all.

The two browser checks that reach the empty state named a budget rather than
reading the control's floor, and when the named budget did not empty the list
they fell back to the two-adult party -- which on this page doubled fifty pence
and still fitted inside a pound. They reported a page defect the page did not
have. They now ask the control what its floor is, which is the gesture a reader
makes, and holds on a page whose cheapest fare is fifty pence and on one whose
cheapest is twelve pounds.

The third file pins what was already true: `atoc_timetable.py` is in
`SOURCE_FILES`, so the timetable cache key covers the code that reads the feed.
Nothing tested it, and a change to how `0000` is read -- the CIF's absent
marker, not midnight -- alters no byte of the download while changing every
calling point the cache holds.

Generated by _cache/make_p2.py, which reads every anchor out of git and off
disk, so an anchor cannot drift from the text it is meant to match. Each region
is bounded by a start and an end line rather than run to the end of the file.
"""

